BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2388@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T102000
SUMMARY:DT bindings and sources shared with U-Boot: current status and ongo
 ing work
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Neil Armstrong · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2388/\n\nSince early 2024\, U-Boot has been synchronizing th
 e DTS and bindings of each Linux release into the U-Boot source tree using
  git subtree functionality. Since this transition\, some platforms like Am
 logic or Qualcomm have immediately switched\, and some older platforms hav
 e been actively migrating to upstream Linux DT sources and bindings while 
 maintaining minimal local U-Boot DTS modifications.\n\nNeil will summarize
  the current state of U-Boot's integration of these upstream DTS\, provide
  an update on ongoing migrations\, and outline the remaining work required
  to fully eliminate local U-Boot DTS modifications.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2388/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2390@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T102000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104000
SUMMARY:Status of the DTS Validation in the Linux Kernel
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Krzysztof Kozlowski · https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2390/\n\nThe great benefit of Devicetree bindings in th
 e current DT schema format is the ability to validate the correctness of D
 TS (Devicetree sources) against those bindings. However\, once validation 
 was introduced\, we discovered that many in-kernel DTS files simply did no
 t pass.\n\nContinuing such summary from 2025\, what is the status of dtbs_
 check now? Which platforms have the most warnings and which are warning-fr
 ee? What improved over last year?\n\nThis is a similar talk to one in 2025
  LPC showing current stage of dtbs_check.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2390/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2469@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T110500
SUMMARY:SDCA and Devicetrees
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Srini Kandagatla · https://lpc.events/event/2
 0/contributions/2469/\n\nLast year's LPC discussion explored whether ACPI-
 defined hardware descriptions could be reused on systems booting with Devi
 cetree\, and where the boundary should exist between the two firmware ecos
 ystems.\n\nSince then\, several related efforts have continued across the 
 kernel community\, having intermediate representation for MIPI DISCO table
 s for SDCA\, emerging ACPI-DT hybrid approaches. At the same time\, new pl
 atform requirements—particularly on ARM64 laptops and embedded systems
 —continue to challenge the traditional "ACPI or DT" model.\n\nThis sessi
 on will review the current state of these efforts\, How should systems wit
 hout ACPI tables should work with drivers like SDCA\, discuss practical us
 e cases where combining ACPI and Devicetree provides value\, examine ongoi
 ng hybrid firmware work\, and identify remaining technical ad architectura
 l challenges. The goal is to gather feedback on whether the community shou
 ld continue evolving hybrid solutions\, standardize common mechanisms\, or
  pursue alternative directions given that we have more data points.\n\nKey
  Highligths:\n- Ongoing efforts.\n- Current Status of work on SDCA.\n- ACP
 I-DT hybrid mode and future.\n- Systems without ACPI tables(mobile devices
 ).\nHans is covering ACPI-DT Hybrid mode in detail https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2363/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2469/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2470@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T111000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T113000
SUMMARY:Streamlining DT Bindings to Protect Upstreaming Momentum
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Amit Kucheria · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2470/\n\nCorporate adoption of an "upstream-first" strategy i
 s gaining traction\, with companies increasingly willing to allocate engin
 eering bandwidth to contribute hardware support directly to the Linux kern
 el. However\, integrating these contributions smoothly remains a practical
  challenge. The review process frequently experiences friction during Devi
 ce Tree (DT) binding discussions\, which can extend timelines\, consume al
 located engineering bandwidth\, and occasionally discourage newcomers to t
 he open-source ecosystem.\n\nThis discussion explores ways to accelerate D
 T binding adoption so developers can maintain their focus on driver implem
 entation. We will discuss current friction points and propose solutions to
  streamline the process. Specifically\, we will evaluate the concept of "e
 xperimental bindings"—exploring whether provisional bindings could be us
 ed under certain circumstances to unblock driver development and maintain 
 contributor momentum without compromising the long-term quality of hardwar
 e descriptions.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2470/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2387@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T122500
SUMMARY:Device Tree Addons: Describe hot-pluggable extension boards
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Hervé Codina · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2387/\n\nDevice Tree has been extremely successful at describ
 ing non-discoverable hardware in Linux and other embedded systems. However
 \, it remains fundamentally monolithic: each Device Tree describes a compl
 ete system\, and reuse across independently developed hardware components 
 is limited.\n\nThis becomes problematic for modular platforms where expans
 ion boards\, mezzanines\, daughter cards\, or hot-pluggable hardware are e
 xpected to work across multiple base boards. While Device Tree Overlays pr
 ovide a mechanism to modify an existing hardware description\, they requir
 e detailed knowledge of the target system. They do not offer a robust way 
 to describe addon boards independently of the base platform nor a way to u
 se the same addon board description to describe the same board connected o
 n multiple connectors available on a single base board.\n\nThe recently pr
 oposed Device Tree Addon format [1] aims to address those limitations. It 
 allows a single addon DTB to describe an extension board that can be used 
 on different connectors of a given base board\, or on different base board
 s\, without requiring board-specific customization.\n\nThe design goal of 
 Device Tree Addon is illustrated in slides used for a talk given at ELCE 2
 025 [2]. The relevant slides sections are the following:\n  - Use case and
  goals\n  - The connector abstraction.\n\nThe "Connector: current status" 
 section available in [2] is now obsolete. The Device Tree Addon concept em
 erged from discussions [3] that followed the ELCE 2025 conference.\n\nThis
  session will briefly present Device Tree Addon concepts recently\nintrodu
 ced and available in the RFC implementation [1]. Beyond introducing the fo
 rmat\, the focus will be on the remaining challenges that must be addresse
 d before broader adoption.\n\nTopics for discussion include remaining desi
 gn questions\, integration with\nDevice Tree bindings and validation rules
 \, standardization efforts in Devicetree Specification (DTSpec). We will a
 lso examine the work still required in dtc\, libfdt\, bootloaders\, and th
 e Linux kernel.\n\nThe goal of the session is to gather feedback from Devi
 ce Tree maintainers\,\nkernel developers\, bootloader developers\, and use
 rs of modular hardware platforms to help shape the future direction of Dev
 ice Tree Addons.\n\nA Bootlin blog post about Device Tree addons (based on
  its current state) is available [4].\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/2
 0260826094950.1088288-1-herve.codina@bootlin.com/\n[2] https://bootlin.com
 /pub/conferences/2025/elce/ceresoli-hotplug-status.pdf\n[3] https://lore.k
 ernel.org/all/20250902105710.00512c6d@booty/\n[4] https://bootlin.com/blog
 /device-tree-addons-describing-non-discoverable-addon-boards-and-connector
 s/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2387/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2389@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T125500
SUMMARY:Using DT overlays to manage a collection of related boards
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Doug Anderson · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2389/\n\nIt is commonplace that many boards in the real world
  have many sibling or cousin boards that are 95-99% the same as each other
 . Some examples:\n\n - During development\, most boards go through several
  revisions. A board might have proto0\, proto1\, evt0\, evt1\, evt1.1\, dv
 t1\, pvt\, and mp revisions. These revisions almost the same with just sma
 ll changes. While only "mp" (mass production) devices should end up in the
  public's hands\, old prototype devices continue to be used for many years
  within the companies supporting the hardware.\n - Many boards have severa
 l small variants that are nearly the same. As an example\, the Pixel 10\, 
 Pixel 10 Pro\, and Pixel 10 Pro XL are based on the same design and are ne
 arly the same.\n - Many boards are based on a reference design and only ma
 ke small changes from that reference design. Chromebooks are one example o
 f this. "sc7180-trogdor" was a reference design and a pile of different OD
 Ms took this design and made small changes. Outside of Chromebooks\, many 
 phones are heavily based on the reference design of the SoC vendor.\n\nPro
 ducts that are highly similar to each other usually end up sharing a singl
 e binary image that is shipped out. That single image contains all needed 
 device trees. Firmware on the product can figure out which specific device
  it's running on and can pick the correct device tree.\n\nThe common pract
 ice in the upstream device tree repository today is that each of these ver
 y similar boards needs its own complete device tree. The device tree is of
 ten shared using #includes of .dtsi files\, but the final result is a pile
  of .dtb files that are mostly the same.\n\nThere are a few problems we ha
 ve today. Two of them that are perhaps the most important:\n\n 1. All of t
 hose device trees take up a lot of space. It would be nice if we didn't ne
 ed a whole pile of .dtb files that are nearly the same.\n 2. While firmwar
 e has all the details about the exact device it's running on\, there is no
  standard way to mark device tree files so firmware can find the right one
 .\n\nThe device-tree overlay concept should help with point #1 in reducing
  the amount of space things take up. Trying to get agreement about how thi
 s should look has been difficult\, though. One question that came up is if
  a "base" device tree was required to be a board or not. For supporting Pi
 xel 10 hardware\, it was most convenient for the base device tree to repre
 sent the SoC itself since some Pixel 10 devices had a different revision o
 f the SoC. A second question that came up is how to deal with the top-leve
 l device-tree "compatible" in this case. It was unclear if we needed some 
 way to "merge" the compatible based on all the overlays included which the
 n led into questions about what needed to be in this top-level "compatible
 " to begin with.\n\nTrying to solve point #2 has come up again and again\,
  but somehow we never end up with any resolution. The typical refrain is t
 hat we need a grand unified design that all device-tree loaders will agree
  to use\, but this always fails to materialize.\n\nBoth problems have down
 stream solutions. As an example\, Pixel phones ship with devicetree overla
 ys to avoid some of the duplication. Device trees and their overlays on Pi
 xel phones have special properties in them that are used to help firmware 
 pick the right one at boot time. Trying to upstream these solutions has me
 t with difficulty though.\n\nLet's have a discussion where we look at prio
 r attempts and see if we can make some more forward progress.\n\nSome thre
 ads I've been involved in (this is by no means a complete list of threads 
 about the topic):\n\n - [[PATCH 1/4] dt-bindings: arm: google: Add binding
 s for frankel/blazer/mustang](https://lore.kernel.org/r/20251111112158.1.I
 72a0b72562b85d02fee424fed939fea9049ddda9@changeid)\n - [[PATCH 4/4] arm64:
  dts: google: Add initial dts for frankel\, blazer\, and mustang](https://
 lore.kernel.org/r/20251111112158.4.I5032910018cdd7d6be7aea78870d04c0dc381d
 6e@changeid)\n - [Proposal: Officially allow "incomplete" trees as a base]
 (https://lore.kernel.org/all/CAD=FV=Ux7nGFnYEyX0cUL-9__BKnTYc+kAJjkF458ZnF
 S7zoJA@mail.gmail.com/)\n - [Proposal: document where SoC info belongs](ht
 tps://lore.kernel.org/r/CAD=FV=W+jE_L_LLgAhD8K_4+CtivSD9-9t7Xe63XuKrKjfyfe
 Q@mail.gmail.com/)
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2389/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2471@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T102220Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T132500
SUMMARY:OS managed Devicetree overlays through UKI add-ons
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Agathe Porte\, Hans de Goede · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2471/\n\nSBCs like the Arduino UNO Q can have 
 a number of extension boards\, like the UNO Media Carrier or an Arduino sh
 ield connected. On top of this extra hardware may be connected through e.g
 . CSI camera connectors and DSI display connectors.\n\nEach of these possi
 ble hardware addons needs a DT overlay to work. This requires some method 
 for the user to select which DT overlays to use.\n\nThere have been severa
 l attempts a decade ago to allow loading DT overlays from Linux userspace 
 for this\, but non has ever been accepted upstream.\n\nUKI addons can prov
 ide an updated DTB\, the purpose of this session is to present and discuss
  a proposal to use this to manage DT overlays from userspace through UKI a
 ddons:\n\nPhase 1: UKI addons with a replacement DTB file embedded\n------
 --------------------------------------------------\n\n- For systems bootin
 g with secureboot\, create DTBs with overlays merged in for popular hardwa
 re addon combinations and build a signed UKI addon for each such DTB on th
 e distros build-system.\n\n- For systems without secureboot any overlay co
 mbination can be supported by building a merged DTB locally and creating a
 n UKI addon from that DTB.\n\nPhase 2: UKI addons with a DTBO file embedde
 d\n---------------------------------------------\n\n- For this phase the U
 KI stub needs to be extended with the capability to merge DTBOs from addon
 s into an earlier loaded DTB\, allowing any combination of overlays by the
  distro providing a signed UKI addon for each available overlay.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2471/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
